//? Title: Summarize survival with the restricted mean survival time
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Report a between-group difference in event-free time up to a fixed horizon

A hazard ratio is a single number only if hazards are proportional. The restricted mean survival time (RMST) needs no such assumption. It is the area under the survival curve up to a horizon \(\tau\),
\[\mu(\tau)=\int_0^\tau S(t)\,dt=E\{\min(T,\tau)\},\]
the expected event-free time over the first \(\tau\) units of follow-up. Its complement \(\tau-\mu(\tau)\) is the restricted mean time lost. The plug-in estimate integrates the Kaplan–Meier curve. Its variance \(\sum_{t_i\le\tau}A_i^2 d_i/\{n_i(n_i-d_i)\}\) weights each event time by the squared area \(A_i\) that remains after it. Groups are compared by the difference in RMST, a number of time units that clinicians can read directly, or by the ratio.

\(\tau\) must be fixed in advance and must lie within the follow-up of both groups. The example shows that the choice matters. It reuses the trial design of the log-rank report: 150 subjects per arm, control hazard 1 and censoring at time 2. Under a constant hazard ratio of 0.6 and a delayed effect (no effect before 0.5, then hazard ratio 0.4), it compares the power of the RMST difference at several horizons with the log-rank test, and checks the coverage of the 95% interval against the exact RMST difference.

```{cpp, id=rmst, file="figures/rmst_power.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/rmst.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/survival_tests.hpp>

#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <random>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

namespace dm = datamunge;
using dm::stats::SurvivalObservation;

// Treatment hazard h1 before time 0.5 and h2 afterwards; control hazard 1.
struct Scenario {
    std::string name;
    double h1, h2;
};

double lifetime(double h1, double h2, std::mt19937_64& random) {
    const double e = std::exponential_distribution<double>(1)(random);
    return e < h1 * .5 ? e / h1 : .5 + (e - h1 * .5) / h2;
}

// Exact RMST of the piecewise-exponential law up to tau.
double true_rmst(double h1, double h2, double tau) {
    const double first = std::min(tau, .5);
    double area = (1 - std::exp(-h1 * first)) / h1;
    if (tau > .5)
        area += std::exp(-h1 * .5) * (1 - std::exp(-h2 * (tau - .5))) / h2;
    return area;
}

int main() {
    std::mt19937_64 random(31);
    const std::vector<Scenario> scenarios{{"Proportional, HR 0.6", .6, .6},
                                          {"Delayed effect", 1, .4}};
    const std::vector<double> horizons{.75, 1, 1.25, 1.5, 1.75, 2};
    const int reps = 2000;
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> power(2);
    std::vector<double> logrank(2);
    std::println("| Scenario | τ | True difference | Mean estimate | Coverage | Power |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (std::size_t s = 0; s < scenarios.size(); ++s) {
        std::vector<int> reject(horizons.size()), cover(horizons.size());
        std::vector<double> mean(horizons.size());
        int logrank_reject = 0;
        for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
            std::vector<SurvivalObservation> records;
            std::vector<int> arm;
            for (int a = 0; a < 2; ++a)
                for (int i = 0; i < 150; ++i) {
                    const auto& sc = scenarios[s];
                    const double t = a == 0 ? lifetime(1, 1, random)
                                            : lifetime(sc.h1, sc.h2, random);
                    records.push_back({std::min(t, 2.0), t <= 2.0});
                    arm.push_back(a);
                }
            logrank_reject += dm::stats::survival_group_test(records, arm).p_value < .05;
            for (std::size_t k = 0; k < horizons.size(); ++k) {
                const auto c = dm::stats::rmst_compare(records, arm, horizons[k]);
                const double truth = true_rmst(scenarios[s].h1, scenarios[s].h2, horizons[k]) -
                                     true_rmst(1, 1, horizons[k]);
                reject[k] += c.difference.p_value < .05;
                cover[k] += c.difference.lower <= truth && truth <= c.difference.upper;
                mean[k] += c.difference.estimate / reps;
            }
        }
        logrank[s] = logrank_reject / double(reps);
        for (std::size_t k = 0; k < horizons.size(); ++k) {
            const double truth = true_rmst(scenarios[s].h1, scenarios[s].h2, horizons[k]) -
                                 true_rmst(1, 1, horizons[k]);
            power[s].push_back(reject[k] / double(reps));
            std::println("| {} | {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", scenarios[s].name,
                         horizons[k], truth, mean[k], cover[k] / double(reps),
                         power[s].back());
        }
    }
    std::println("\nLog-rank power: {:.3f} (proportional), {:.3f} (delayed effect).",
                 logrank[0], logrank[1]);

    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.line(horizons, power[0], "RMST difference, proportional", {26, 90, 147}, 2)
        .line(horizons, power[1], "RMST difference, delayed effect", {176, 72, 43}, 2)
        .line({.75, 2}, {logrank[0], logrank[0]}, "Log-rank, proportional", {140, 170, 205},
              1.2)
        .line({.75, 2}, {logrank[1], logrank[1]}, "Log-rank, delayed effect", {225, 160, 140},
              1.2)
        .title("Power of the RMST difference by horizon, 150 per arm")
        .x_label("Horizon τ")
        .y_label("Rejection rate at 5%")
        .ylim(0, 1.35)
        .size(700, 420);
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    plot.save_svg("build/rmst_power.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Scenario | τ | True difference | Mean estimate | Coverage | Power |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Proportional, HR 0.6 | 0.75 | 0.0763 | 0.0751 | 0.961 | 0.749 |
| Proportional, HR 0.6 | 1 | 0.1199 | 0.1184 | 0.956 | 0.838 |
| Proportional, HR 0.6 | 1.25 | 0.1659 | 0.1643 | 0.954 | 0.890 |
| Proportional, HR 0.6 | 1.5 | 0.2122 | 0.2105 | 0.954 | 0.926 |
| Proportional, HR 0.6 | 1.75 | 0.2572 | 0.2555 | 0.958 | 0.948 |
| Proportional, HR 0.6 | 2 | 0.3000 | 0.2982 | 0.958 | 0.965 |
| Delayed effect | 0.75 | 0.0101 | 0.0094 | 0.946 | 0.068 |
| Delayed effect | 1 | 0.0362 | 0.0356 | 0.950 | 0.144 |
| Delayed effect | 1.25 | 0.0730 | 0.0726 | 0.950 | 0.274 |
| Delayed effect | 1.5 | 0.1165 | 0.1165 | 0.947 | 0.443 |
| Delayed effect | 1.75 | 0.1639 | 0.1644 | 0.949 | 0.598 |
| Delayed effect | 2 | 0.2130 | 0.2140 | 0.950 | 0.721 |

Log-rank power: 0.978 (proportional), 0.906 (delayed effect).
\image(figures/rmst_power.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Power of the 95% RMST-difference test by horizon τ for 150 subjects per arm, under a constant hazard ratio of 0.6 (blue) and a delayed effect (orange). The light horizontal lines give the log-rank power in each scenario. 2000 trials per point.)
\alttext(Both RMST power curves rise with the horizon. The blue proportional-hazards curve goes from about 0.75 to 0.97 and meets its light-blue log-rank line near the largest horizon. The orange delayed-effect curve starts near 0.07 and reaches about 0.72 at τ = 2, still well below its light-orange log-rank line at about 0.91.)

The RMST difference is unbiased at every horizon, and its 95% Wald interval covers the exact difference 94.6–96.1% of the time. Power depends strongly on \(\tau\), because the difference accumulates only where the survival curves have separated. Under proportional hazards it rises from 75% at \(\tau=0.75\) to 97% at \(\tau=2\), the end of follow-up, where it matches the log-rank test. Under the delayed effect the first half time unit contributes nothing, and power at \(\tau=0.75\) is barely above the 5% level. Even at \(\tau=2\) it is 72%, against 91% for the log-rank test.

RMST is therefore not a power device for delayed effects. Here it is less powerful than the log-rank test because it gives full weight to the early period with no difference. Its advantage is interpretability. "Treated patients lived on average 0.21 time units longer during the first 2 units of follow-up" remains true and meaningful when hazards cross, whereas a single hazard ratio averages incompatible effects. A horizon near the end of reliable follow-up in both arms, fixed before seeing the data, gives the most information. Choosing \(\tau\) after looking at the curves invalidates the test.

Per-arm RMST with standard errors and intervals, and the difference, RMST ratio and RMTL ratio with intervals and p-values, all match survRM2's `rmst2` on the `lung` data (by sex, at two horizons and 90% confidence) and on the `veteran` data (by treatment).

[Royston and Parmar (2013)|https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2288-13-152] · [Uno et al. (2014)|https://doi.org/10.1200/JCO.2013.53.2149]
